ets talked about in pretty  reductionist ways either more immigrants  are good or more immigrants are bad we  need more if they're breaking the law  what's the biggest single thing that you  as an immigration reporter who have  covered this for years what's the  biggest single thing that you think  people are missing from the immigration  debate I was in right now and this has  changed over time but right now there's  a certain assumption that people are  either legitimate refugees who are  fleeing some particular form of  persecution or they are just coming to  work and the reality is that people are  never just coming to people are never  just settle you in another country for  one reason right there might be one  specific thing that makes them decide  that it's the right time but they're  coming they they may want to work they  may be coming because they're worried  about they're not worried about their  own safety but they're worried about the  safety of their children there may be a  particular threat that they're fleeing  which may itself might be at the hands  of the government might be at the hands  of criminals might be something between  the two if people were just lumps of  Labor going back and forth it would be  much easier to retain the legal work  system that we have right now which kind  of assumes the people will be here for a  couple of years and then they'll go back  that's not how people work they you know  integrate into communities they put down  roots and so because you can't easily  isolate why people are moving or why  they're staying  it makes the whole system much harder  does  thank Darland is an immigration reporter  at ProPublica alright thank you so much  for joining us thank you
 

